# Population and Settlements: How People Fill the Earth

> Eight billion people now share the planet, crowded into a handful of
> rich regions and scattered across vast empty ones. This lesson
> explains the growth, the patterns, the migration between them, and
> the settlements people build.

## 1. A Story in Milestones

| Milestone | Year | Gap from previous |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 billion | ~1804 | — |
| 2 billion | 1927 | 123 years |
| 3 billion | 1959 | 32 years |
| 4 billion | 1974 | 15 years |
| 7 billion | 2011 | 12 years |
| 8 billion | November 2022 | 11 years |

[note]
Although each billion arrives sooner, the *rate* of growth peaked
around the late 1960s at above 2% per year and has fallen to under 1%:
the world is still growing, but ever more slowly.
[/note]

## 2. The Demographic Transition Model

[definition]
The **demographic transition model** traces a population from high
birth and death rates to low ones as a country develops, passing
through five stages.
[/definition]

| Stage | Birth rate | Death rate | Growth | Example today |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 High stationary | High | High | Nearly zero | A few isolated groups only |
| 2 Early expanding | High | Falling fast | Rapid | Parts of Africa |
| 3 Late expanding | Falling | Low | Slowing | India, Latin America |
| 4 Low stationary | Low | Low | Nearly zero | USA, much of Europe |
| 5 Declining | Very low | Low | Negative | Japan, Germany |

## 3. Where People Live

Asia holds about **60% of humanity**. Within it, population presses
thickest on the river plains and coastlines of East, South, and
South-East Asia, in western Europe, and along the north-eastern United
States.

